WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.969
-You know our first guest
tonight from movies such as

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.171
"Gods and Monsters,"
"George of the Jungle,"

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.439
and "The Mummy."

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.808
He is receiving critical acclaim
for his starring role in

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:10.243
"The Whale,"
which is in theaters now,

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:13.279
Please welcome to the show,
Brendan Fraser, everybody.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.281
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:24.257 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:33.232 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.034
-Welcome to the show, Brendan.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.635
-Thank you.
-This has been --

00:00:36.635 --> 00:00:40.339
We have not seen each other
since 2008 when we collaborated.

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.342
-Yes.
[ laughter ]

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.476
-On --On "Journey to the
Center of the Earth."

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:44.944
It was --it was very --

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.780
I'd never been anywhere near
a movie with a budget that big.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:48.914
It was very exciting
to be there.

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:50.816
You were incredibly gracious
and kind.

00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:51.884
-Well, thank you.

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:53.386
-You're very welcome.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.888
I don't feel like I was given
much to do in the film.

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:57.089
-You were the bad guy,
weren't you?

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:58.858
-I was the bad guy.
-The defect-- defector.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.793
-I always said I had two
scenes in the movie.

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.428
The first one was --
I was like,

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:03.796
"You can't go to
the center of the Earth."

00:01:03.796 --> 00:01:05.297
and then at the end,
I was like, "Whoa!"

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:07.266
-"Whoa!"

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:09.102
-But it was 3-D.
-It was.

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.437
-And it was --the technology was
sort of ahead of its time.

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:12.838
-It was.
It was the tip of the spear

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:15.874
for 3-D tech as we see it
now in theaters,

00:01:15.874 --> 00:01:17.310
because the cameras we used
were the ones that

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.246
James Cameron kind of beta
tested with that movie.

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:23.183
And then, we --
we put them under water

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:24.550
and dropped them off
of helicopters.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:25.718
And then he took them
to New Zealand.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.053
he shot "Avatar" with them.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:29.488
And people talk about both
those movies the same.

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:31.757
[ laughter and applause ]

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:34.127
but I do --
I do want to show my one --

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.596
one clip because
it was a 3-D movie.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.832
I wanted to make sure
I had one 3-D moment.

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.432
Again, it's an action film.

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:41.434
It was an action film.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.236
I had none of the action scenes,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.405
but I'm very proud of this
very short clip.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.539
-It's good.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.774
-This is the wrong time
to shut us down.

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:50.810
I have got seismic activity
in Bolivia.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.779
I've got a 13 millimeter
shift in Mongolia.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.882
Allen, you can't shut us down.
keep the lab open.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.650
It's all I've got left of max.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.287
-It's been 10 years
since Max, Trevor.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:02.822
I am sorry about all this.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.557
I really am.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.994
-I mean -- whoa.
-Woo!

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:12.231
-I mean, if you -- if you were
in the theater opening night,

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:13.833
people screamed.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.001
[ laughter ]

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:18.271
Now you also --
another face you haven't seen

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:21.274
for a very long
time is our cue card guy, Wally.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:22.475
Do you see Wally over there?

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.243
Do you recognize old Wally?
Cue Card Wally?

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:25.845
-Hey, Wally!
-Hey, how ya doing?

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:26.979
-How have you been?

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:28.815
-I'm great, man.
I'm really good.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.583
-It's good to see you again.
-I know. you too, buddy.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.318
-So, Wally, of course,
was the cue card guy

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.853
when you hosted "SNL."
-Yes.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:36.022
-And another clip
I'd like to show --

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:37.690
because I remember watching
when it happened --

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.826
you did a "Xena:
A Warrior Princess" skit.

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.427
-Yes, I did.
-With Molly Shannon.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.495
-Correct.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:44.464
-And one of those things
that, you know,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.132
happens in live
television happened.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.400
And I want to say -- I'm not
going to say what it is,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.670
but I want to say you rolled
with the punches very well.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.438
You fixed the problem
very quickly.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:53.673
-Thank you.
-Can we show it real quick?

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.141
-Please do.
-All right. Here you go.

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.177
[ screaming ]

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:04.417
[ laughter and applause ]

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.390
[ Screaming continues ]

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.394
-Xena, you are truly
a warrior Princess!

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:16.295
[ laughter ]

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:17.630
It's really well done.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.765
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:22.568
-Not just the awareness
to know your wig came off,

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:25.004
but to put it back on,
and then to split the hair.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.372
that's --
-Thank you.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.507
And then I looked up,
and I was too busy laughing,

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:29.742
and I see Wally,
and he is doing --

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:32.244
[ laughter ]

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:34.246
-There you go.
Wally keeps everybody on task.

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:35.615
That's his job.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:40.486
This is such an incredibly
heartbreaking and wonderful

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:42.321
performance in this film,
"The Whale,"

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:46.492
and you've been receiving
deserved accolades for it.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:49.462
you play a 600-pound man
who is housebound,

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:50.797
trying to reconnect
with his daughter.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.030
-Yeah.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:55.301
-What drew you to doing --
what you must have known

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.036
it would have
a huge emotional toll --

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:58.571
sorry, toll to --

00:03:58.571 --> 00:03:59.872
-When I read it,
I felt like I could

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.641
be friends with this guy.
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:05.778
-I identified in many ways about
him as a father and as him

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:08.214
wanting to reach out to young
people to get them to be honest

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.982
about who they --
who they are.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:15.788
And I also felt that there was
a real story to be told about

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:18.124
someone who's locked behind
closed doors.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:21.360
and we get to peer inside his
world, which is really just

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.962
a two-bedroom apartment.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.697
That, and of course, it was
directed by Darren Aronofsky.

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:26.198
-Yeah, he is wonderful.

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:29.535
-And I -- I absolutely felt --
well, you know, "sign me up."

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:33.972
And on top of that, there was
an extensive and careful use

00:04:33.972 --> 00:04:36.842
of prosthetic makeup,
which I'm a big fan of.

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:39.212
I love the craft of filmmaking

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.514
and all of the elements
that go into it.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:46.285
And -- and a long time
collaborator of Darren's,

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:51.090
Adrien Morot, had put
together a character look that

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:56.429
I'm pretty sure for the first
time obeyed the laws of physics

00:04:56.429 --> 00:05:01.300
and gravity in how it responded
to an actor inside of it.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:05.371
Very often weight gain suits
and obesity portrayed in films

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.706
has -- has been unfair.

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:12.144
It's been a silhouette of --
of a costume

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:17.616
inside an actor who is quite fit
just seems to --

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:22.722
just reorients the way
that we believe that --

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:26.291
that someone can actually
move and exist.

00:05:26.291 --> 00:05:29.128
And in this -- in this
incarnation of it,

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:35.534
Charlie is a man who has
significant health challenges,

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:39.171
and he has very little time
to reconnect with his daughter,

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:42.208
if he can even do that at all.

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:45.611
His is a path of redemption,
that he seeks.

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:47.313
His daughter is played by
Sadie Sink,

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:49.648
who is fantastic in this film.

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:51.484
I hope you get
a chance to see her.

00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:55.187
And his -- his friend and
caretaker is played by

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:59.925
Hong Chau, who is brilliant
in this -- this role.

00:05:59.925 --> 00:06:01.761
She is great in everything
she does.

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.362
There is something
wonderful about Hong

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.264
that I noticed right away.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:11.204
She's -- has this ability
to say more in between lines

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.605
of dialogue and in the pauses
and the silences

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:16.008
very often than what the lines
are that are on the page.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:20.612
And it was a -- a piece that was
shot during COVID.

00:06:20.612 --> 00:06:23.449
And, so, for that, clearly, we
had to be very careful

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:27.819
with one another
for our well-being, our safety.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:31.523
And somehow that translates
to what I think you see

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:32.892
on the screen.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:38.464
We -- we seem to be giving it
everything that we have

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:41.933
because of, well, the very real
existential threat that

00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:43.769
we might not be able
to come back and do this later.

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:46.472
So, might as well leave it
all on the screen.

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.474
-And I think one of the
remarkable things about,

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:52.511
obviously, the time that goes
into prosthetics is any --

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:55.747
if you see any seam, if
you see anything that takes you

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:57.883
out of the story, it won't have
the same resonance.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:07:00.786
And, so, I'm -- I was really
taken aback, just appreciating.

00:07:00.786 --> 00:07:01.987
-Yes.
-And it's nice you say

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:03.288
there are so many
different people that go into

00:07:03.288 --> 00:07:04.423
the making of a movie.

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:05.824
This is one in particular
where you realize,

00:07:05.824 --> 00:07:08.327
"Oh, we're seeing craftsmanship
on the screen here."

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:11.063
Because at no moment do you
think,

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.164
"Oh, this is just, you know --

00:07:12.164 --> 00:07:14.299
-It's --
it's an analog creation.

00:07:14.299 --> 00:07:16.301
There's no CGU -- well, with
the exception of maybe --

00:07:16.301 --> 00:07:19.605
on some fabric that was in its
own movie that morning

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:20.272
or something, you know?

00:07:20.272 --> 00:07:22.074
A little bit later.

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:24.544
But aside from that, what you
see is what you get.

00:07:24.544 --> 00:07:26.445
That's, you know, an actor in --

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:31.584
in a suit that is cumbersome
enough to drive home

00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:36.588
the authenticity of what
it appears to be

00:07:36.588 --> 00:07:39.959
an individual or a person who
lives with that challenge.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:41.894
-Well, it was a stunning
thing to see on film.

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:43.228
I have a lot more to ask you
about.

00:07:43.228 --> 00:07:45.230
We will be right back with more
from Brendan Fraser,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:46.232
everybody.

